[center][b][size=4]This is a framework, not a mod you play. If another mod told you to install it, turn it on and you're done. Everything below is for mod authors.[/size][/b]

[/center]

[line]


Harbinger is a control layer for the AI that drives your NPCs. When more than one mod wants to steer the same actor, Harbinger decides which mod owns which part of it at any moment, so they run together instead of overwriting each other.


The important word is [i]part[/i]. Control is split by facet, not by actor. One mod can own where a follower walks while another keeps running his combat, a third holds his dialogue, and a fourth manages his gear, all on the same body at the same time. Harbinger only ever arbitrates the exact facet two mods actually disagree on, and it leaves everything else running untouched. Nothing gets a blanket lock and no mod is switched off wholesale.


That precision is the whole point. When a facet is contended, Harbinger routes it to the owner and quietly withholds only the competing input, at the point the NPC would receive it, so the other mod's scripts and behaviour stay alive the entire time. The moment the owner releases, the facet is handed straight back with nothing left behind. A mod loses control of one thing for as long as something else needs it, and not a scrap more.


Harbinger owns [i]whether[/i] a piece of control reaches the NPC. Your mod owns [i]what[/i] that control is. So it never generates behaviour of its own and never fights the engine. It shapes what the NPC is allowed to receive, and the engine and the client do the rest.


[size=5][b]For players[/b][/size]


You install it because something else needs it. There is nothing to configure and no ESP to sort. It edits no records and adds no scripts to your NPCs, and with no client mod asking for anything it does nothing at all. An NPC that no mod is currently steering behaves exactly as it would without Harbinger.


[size=5][b]For mod authors[/b][/size]


Harbinger is a C ABI you talk to from your own SKSE plugin. You claim a single facet on an actor, aggression, combat target, casting, a package, and so on, at a priority you choose. You keep driving the NPC with your own code. Harbinger's job is to make the engine and the other mods let that specific facet through, and to hold everything you did not claim out of your way.


Claims stack cleanly. Two mods claiming the same facet resolve by priority, the higher one owns it, the other waits, and it reverts the instant the last claim is dropped. Two mods claiming [i]different[/i] facets on the same NPC simply both work.


Some facets are proven in the field already, in fact this mod was created to facilitate the advanced features of [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/187445]marth Follower Overhaul[/url], which is a full Harbinger client and the worked example. Others are built and ready but a mod author would be the first to run them in earnest. The page is honest about which is which. The full facet list, the proven-versus-untested status of each, and the header you build against are in the [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/APMF]GitHub repo[/url].


[size=5][b]Coexistence[/b][/size]


Running alongside other mods is not a feature here, it is the entire design, so it is a hard rule and not a hope. Harbinger only ever hooks in ways that chain with other mods, it reads the engine's own answer before it changes anything, and it verifies what it is hooking at install. It withholds a single competing input and never a whole system. If it cannot do a thing safely it declines that one thing, it does not crash your game or disturb anything else on the actor.


[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]


[list]

[*]SKSE64[/*]

[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/*]

[*]No DLC required.[/*]

[/list]

Skyrim 1.6.1170, 1.5.97 and 1.7.104 are supported. On 1.7.104 you install nothing extra. There is no Address Library for it, so Harbinger carries its own address table inside the DLL.


[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]


Install it with your mod manager like any other SKSE plugin. There is no plugin file to enable and load order does not matter. Launch through SKSE.


[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]


[b]I was sent here by another mod. Do I need to set anything up?[/b]

No. Install it, turn it on, launch through SKSE. It has no MCM and nothing to tune.


[b]Will it conflict with my follower framework or AI overhaul?[/b]

No, that is what it is for. It edits no records and no AI packages, and it holds back only the exact input a claiming mod is overriding, so the rest of your other mods keep running on the same NPC.


[b]Is it safe to add or remove mid-playthrough?[/b]

Yes. Its state lives in the SKSE co-save and reverts cleanly.


[b]I'm a mod author. Where do I start?[/b]

The header and the integration notes are in the [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/APMF]GitHub repo[/url]. You fetch one exported function after SKSE load, keep the pointer, and call it from your own plugin. It is a small surface.


[b]Does my client mod have to be written in C++?[/b]

Yes for now. Harbinger is a C ABI between SKSE plugins, so a client is a native plugin. A Papyrus-facing layer is not part of this release.


[size=5][b]STATUS[/b][/size]

   

 Harbinger lets a mod take control of one thing an NPC is doing without taking over the NPC. The NPC keeps running its own behaviour. A mod borrows one piece of it, and hands it back when it is done. That is the whole idea.


 It works on any NPC, not just followers. Followers are simply what the first mod using it happens to control.


 You do not configure it. Install it and the mods that need it will use it.


[b] What Works[/b]


  Fully Tested


 - Making an NPC cast a spell a mod chose, with the game's own animation(in Combat, Out of combat still requires work, though the spell will fire with no NPC anim). Heals and attack spells both.

 - Choosing which spell an NPC reaches for, and holding the hand so the AI cannot swap it mid cast.

 - Deciding who an NPC fights. This one runs in every fight already.

 - Stopping an NPC using particular kinds of attack, cleanly, without breaking its behaviour.

 - Controlling how fast an NPC moves, and it holds even on an NPC locked into a package.

 - Controlling how an NPC feels about people, and how easily it notices them.

 - Making an NPC crouch, and making it look at something.

 - Handing an NPC a job the game will actually run, like walking to a body.


 [b]Built but untested[/b]


 Compiled and should work. Never run in a real game.


 - Stopping an NPC moving entirely. Drawing or sheathing a weapon. Interrupting dialogue. Playing a one off animation. Equipping or unequipping worn gear. Claiming a shout.


[b] Unimplemented[/b]


 - Weapons. Harbinger can hold a hand against spells. It cannot stop the AI equipping a different weapon there.

 - Re aiming a cast once it starts. Changing target means dropping the claim and starting over.

 - Blocking another mod from taking a shout or a combat target. Harbinger records who should own them, it does not enforce it.

                    


 How finished is this


 This is a beta. Only the three items under "what works" have been seen working in a real game. Everything else is unproven, and most of what changed in this release has had very little time in game. Expect rough edges and please

 report them with a log.                                       


This is deep engine work and it is new. The parts that are proven are proven because a shipping mod leans on them every session. The parts that are not, I have marked as not. If you build against it and something is wrong, tell me and I will fix it.